WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.391
Axum extractors are one of the framework's
strengths because they help handlers

2
00:00:08.471 --> 00:00:12.648
receive request data in structured forms.

3
00:00:12.728 --> 00:00:20.502
Path parameters, query strings, headers,
JSON bodies, forms, cookies, state,

4
00:00:20.582 --> 00:00:25.340
and other inputs can be pulled into typed
values.

5
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:33.078
That structure is valuable because it
reduces ad hoc parsing and makes handler

6
00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:37.916
signatures communicate what the route
expects.

7
00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:42.057
Extraction is not the same as validation.

8
00:00:42.137 --> 00:00:48.751
A value can parse successfully and still
be wrong for the operation.

9
00:00:48.831 --> 00:00:54.401
A path identifier may exist but belong to
another tenant.

10
00:00:54.481 --> 00:01:01.211
A JSON field may have the correct type but
be outside an allowed range.

11
00:01:01.291 --> 00:01:05.120
A header may be present but not trusted.

12
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:12.859
A request body may be valid JSON but too
large or too expensive to process for the

13
00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:13.635
route.

14
00:01:13.715 --> 00:01:20.213
Defensive Axum applications validate input
at clear boundaries.

15
00:01:20.293 --> 00:01:24.122
They use domain types where practical,

16
00:01:24.202 --> 00:01:30.004
so validated values are different from raw
request values.

17
00:01:30.084 --> 00:01:34.726
They reject unsupported values
consistently,

18
00:01:34.806 --> 00:01:39.447
normalize only when that behavior is
explicit,

19
00:01:39.527 --> 00:01:46.605
and avoid letting raw strings drift deep
into sensitive business logic.

20
00:01:46.685 --> 00:01:54.228
A handler should make it clear when data
has moved from parsed input to trusted

21
00:01:54.308 --> 00:01:56.280
application value.

22
00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:04.251
Body limits and parsing expectations
matter because web clients are untrusted.

23
00:02:04.331 --> 00:02:12.337
A service should not let an arbitrary
request force excessive memory allocation,

24
00:02:12.417 --> 00:02:16.827
expensive parsing, or long processing
time.

25
00:02:16.907 --> 00:02:23.173
Reviewers should understand the configured
body-size limits,

26
00:02:23.253 --> 00:02:30.331
which routes accept large payloads, which
content types are supported,

27
00:02:30.411 --> 00:02:35.285
and how parsing failures are returned to
clients.

28
00:02:35.365 --> 00:02:39.890
Safe rejection behavior is part of the
design.

29
00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:48.325
Validation failures should stop work
before sensitive operations, data retrieval,

30
00:02:48.405 --> 00:02:50.261
or state mutation.

31
00:02:50.341 --> 00:02:57.768
The response should be consistent and
useful without revealing internals.

32
00:02:57.848 --> 00:03:06.434
The operational logs should capture enough
context for investigation without storing

33
00:03:06.514 --> 00:03:11.620
secrets, raw tokens, or unnecessary
personal data.
